# Bakmark environment variables.
#
# Copy to .env for local use:   cp env.dist .env
# Compose reads .env automatically; start.sh exports its own defaults and only
# sets what is missing, so a value here wins for anything compose interpolates.
#
# Nothing in here is a secret: the code holds no credentials, because mail goes
# out from our own Postfix and the list is a SQLite file. If that ever changes,
# the secret does not belong in this file.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Which environment this container is. start.sh only.
#
#   local       defaults: ./data          on 8085
#   staging     defaults: ./data-staging  on 8086
#   production  no defaults: set BAKMARK_DATA_DIR and BAKMARK_PORT below
#
# Every environment reads the same variables through the same code path. The
# only difference is which of them a deployment supplies versus which get a
# default for a throwaway local run.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BAKMARK_ENV=local

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Data
#
# The subscriber list. One file per environment; staging must never open the
# production file. compose.yaml has no default for this, so a bare
# `docker compose up` fails instead of guessing.
#
#   production on the VPS:  /var/lib/bakmark
#   staging on the VPS:     /var/lib/bakmark-staging
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BAKMARK_DATA_DIR=./data

# Names the compose project, so two environments on one host cannot `down`
# each other's containers. deploy.sh sets it per environment.
# COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME=bakmark-local

# Inside the container; the bind mount above provides the directory.
# Only change this if you also change the mount.
# SUBSCRIBERS_DB=/data/subscribers.sqlite

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Ports, all published on 127.0.0.1 only. Caddy on the host is the public door.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BAKMARK_PORT=8085
# MAILPIT_UI_PORT=8025
# MAILPIT_SMTP_PORT=1025

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public URL
#
# Used for the links in the confirmation mail. If this is wrong, the mails go
# out with dead links, which is worse than not sending at all. Set it to the
# address a visitor actually types, including on staging.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SITE_URL=http://localhost:8085

# 1 = search engines may index this site; anything else means noindex + a
# robots.txt that refuses everything. Production sets 1, staging never does.
# Unset is deliberately the non-indexable case: forgetting it makes a site
# invisible, which is recoverable, instead of putting a staging copy in Google.
SITE_INDEXABLE=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Mail
#
# Development defaults to the Mailpit catcher at smtp://mailpit:1025 and the
# code refuses any non-loopback host unless MAIL_ALLOW_REMOTE=1, so a copied
# .env cannot mail a real person from a laptop.
#
# Staging and production both send REAL mail through the host's Postfix on
# 127.0.0.1:25. Staging stays safe through SIGNUP_ALLOWED_DOMAINS, not through
# a catcher: mail that never arrives cannot tell you whether SPF, DKIM or the
# content work. deploy.sh sets this per environment and overrides this file.
#
# Without SMTP_URL, production refuses to send.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SMTP_URL=smtp://mailpit:1025
# Must be at news.bakmark.nl: OpenDKIM's SigningTable covers that domain, and a
# From elsewhere leaves the message unsigned (mail-tester: "not signed with DKIM").
MAIL_FROM=Bakmark <noreply@news.bakmark.nl>

# Escape hatch for a real SMTP host outside production. Leave unset.
# MAIL_ALLOW_REMOTE=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signup allowlist
#
# Comma-separated domains. Empty means no restriction (local and production).
# start.sh sets this automatically when BAKMARK_ENV=staging, so a staging run
# cannot start with an open form by forgetting the variable.
#   SIGNUP_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=markmeijerman.eu,mail-tester.com
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SIGNUP_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=
